    <div class="alpheios-lex-entry" lemma-id="n31899" body-key=""><b><span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">obstē^trīco</span></b>  (<b><span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">opst-</span></b> ), āre,
			v. n. and <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>I.</b><i>a.</i> [id.], <i>to perform the office
				of a midwife</i> (eccl. Lat.), <i><span class="en">Tert. ad
				Nat. 2, 12</span></i>: 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">Hebraeas (sc. mulieres),</span>”
				<i>to assist in childbirth</i>,
				 <i><span class="en">Vulg.
					Exod. 1, 16</span></i>
			: 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">obstetricandi scientia,</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">id. ib. 1, 19</span></i>
			.—</div>
        <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense2"><b>B.</b>
			<b>Trop.</b>: 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">poëtis obstetricantibus,</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">Tert. ad Nat. 2, 2</span></i>
			.</div>
